  "live_records": {
    "q-40108": {
      "dom_id": "step-q40108",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "If $u=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,,$ $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]\\,,$ and $u\\cdot u=17,$ then $\\frac{u\\cdot (2u)}{u\\cdot (\\frac{1}{3}v)}=$",
      "worked_solution": "We will use the fact that the dot product is linear.\n\nTo quickly compute the numerator $u\\cdot (2u),$ we multiply $u\\cdot u$ by $2.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot (2u) & =2(u\\cdot u) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 17 \\\\  & =34.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo quickly compute the denominator $u\\cdot (\\frac{1}{3}v),$ we compute $u\\cdot v$ first and then multiply the result by $\\frac{1}{3}.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot (\\frac{1}{3}v) & =\\frac{1}{3}({u}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}+{u}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}+{u}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{3}+{u}_{4}\\cdot {v}_{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}((0)\\cdot (1)+3\\cdot (-4)+2\\cdot 2+2\\cdot (-5)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(0-12+4-10) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-18) \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{u\\cdot (2u)}{u\\cdot (\\frac{1}{3}v)}=\\frac{34}{-6}=-\\frac{17}{3}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
