    "q-74040": {
      "dom_id": "step-q74040",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "If $u=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\,,$ $v=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,,$ and $u\\cdot u=27,$ then $\\frac{(2u)\\cdot u}{u\\cdot (3v)}=$",
      "worked_solution": "We will use the fact that the dot product is linear.\n\nTo quickly compute the numerator $(2u)\\cdot u,$ we multiply $u\\cdot u$ by $2.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2u)\\cdot u & =2(u\\cdot u) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 27 \\\\  & =54.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo quickly compute the denominator $u\\cdot (3v),$ we compute $u\\cdot v$ first and then multiply the result by $3.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot (3v) & =3(u\\cdot v) \\\\  & =3({u}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}+{u}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}+{u}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{3}+{u}_{4}\\cdot {v}_{4}) \\\\  & =3(2\\cdot (-3)+3\\cdot (-2)+1\\cdot (-1)+(-2)\\cdot 3+(-3)\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =3(-6-6-1-6-6) \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-25) \\\\  & =-75.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{(2u)\\cdot u}{u\\cdot (3v)}=\\frac{54}{-75}=-\\frac{18}{25}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
