    "q-39195": {
      "dom_id": "step-q39195",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "If $u=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\,,$ $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\\,,$ and $u\\cdot v=-22,$ then $\\frac{u\\cdot (2v)}{u\\cdot (5u)}=$",
      "worked_solution": "We will use the fact that the dot product is linear.\n\nTo quickly compute the numerator $u\\cdot (2v),$ we multiply $u\\cdot v$ by $2.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot (2v) & =2(u\\cdot v) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-22) \\\\  & =-44.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo quickly compute the denominator $u\\cdot (5u),$ we compute $u\\cdot u$ first and then multiply the result by $5.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot (5u) & =5(u\\cdot u) \\\\  & =5({u}_{1}^{2}+{u}_{2}^{2}+{u}_{3}^{2}+{u}_{4}^{2}) \\\\  & =5((-2)^{2}+{3}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{4}^{2}) \\\\  & =5(4+9+1+16) \\\\  & =5\\cdot 30 \\\\  & =150.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{u\\cdot (2v)}{u\\cdot (5u)}=\\frac{-44}{150}=-\\frac{22}{75}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
