    "q-40114": {
      "dom_id": "step-q40114",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "If $\\alpha \\beta =12$ and $a\\cdot b=4$, then what is $(\\alpha b)\\cdot (\\beta a)-21?$",
      "worked_solution": "Using the fact that the dot product is linear in both variables, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha b)\\cdot (\\beta a)-21 & =\\alpha (b\\cdot \\beta a)-21 \\\\  & =\\alpha \\beta (b\\cdot a)-21.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, using that the dot product is symmetric, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha \\beta (b\\cdot a)-21 & =\\alpha \\beta (a\\cdot b)-21.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the values for $\\alpha \\beta $ and $a\\cdot b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha \\beta (a\\cdot b)-21 & =12\\cdot (4)-21 \\\\  & =48-21 \\\\  & =27\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
