    "q-40051": {
      "dom_id": "step-q40051",
      "name": "Question 10",
      "problem": "Suppose ${b}_{2}=p{b}_{1}+a$ and ${b}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{2}=0.$ If $a=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ and ${b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]$, find the value of $p.$ \n\n*Hint: You might wish to take the equation ${b}_{2}=p{b}_{1}+a$ and find the dot product of both sides with ${b}_{1}.$*",
      "worked_solution": "First, we take the equation ${b}_{2}=p{b}_{1}+a$ and find the dot product of both sides with ${b}_{1}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{2} & =p{b}_{1}+a \\\\ {b}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{2} & ={b}_{1}\\cdot (p{b}_{1}+a) \\\\ {b}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{2} & ={b}_{1}\\cdot (p{b}_{1})+({b}_{1}\\cdot a) \\\\ {b}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{2} & =p({b}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{1})+({b}_{1}\\cdot a)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the formula for the dot product to compute the values of ${b}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{1}\\cdot a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{1} & ={b}_{1}^{2}+{b}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & ={2}^{2}+(-3)^{2} \\\\  & =4+9 \\\\  & =13 \\\\ {b}_{1}\\cdot a & ={b}_{1}{a}_{1}+{b}_{2}{a}_{2} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2+(-3)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =4+3 \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the values for ${b}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{1},$ ${b}_{1}\\cdot a,$ and ${b}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{2} & =p({b}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{1})+({b}_{1}\\cdot a) \\\\ 0 & =p\\cdot 13+7 \\\\ -13p & =7 \\\\ p & =-\\frac{7}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
